/* NCERT Chemistry Class XI — Chapter 5: Thermodynamics (book page 135–167)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'chem-11-05',
  subj: 'chem',
  cls: 11,
  no: 5,
  title: 'Thermodynamics',
  src: 'NCERT Chemistry XI · Unit 5',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: 'Introduction', t: 'Chemical energy stored in molecules is released as heat in reactions (fuel combustion), converted to mechanical energy (fuel in engine) or electrical energy (galvanic cell). Thermodynamics studies these energy transformations. Laws of thermodynamics deal with macroscopic systems in equilibrium (or moving between equilibrium states), not with how or how fast changes occur.' },
    { id: 'sy1', topic: '5.1.1 System and surroundings (Fig 5.1)', t: 'A system is the part of the universe under observation; the rest is the surroundings: universe = system + surroundings. Practically, surroundings are the portion of the universe that can interact with the system. The wall (real or imaginary) separating system and surroundings is the boundary.' },
    { id: 'sy2', topic: '5.1.2 Types of system (Fig 5.2)', t: 'Open system: exchange of both energy and matter with surroundings (reactants in an open beaker). Closed system: exchange of energy but not matter (reactants in a closed vessel of conducting material). Isolated system: no exchange of energy or matter (reactants in a thermos flask or insulated closed vessel).' },
    { id: 'sy3', topic: '5.1.3 State of the system — state functions', t: 'A macroscopic system is described by state variables such as pressure p, volume V, temperature T, amount n. State functions (state variables) depend only on the state of the system and not on how the state was reached: p, V, T, U, H, S, G. A gas at a given T, p, V, n has a definite state regardless of history.' },
    { id: 'ie1', topic: '5.1.4 Internal energy U', t: 'Internal energy U is the total energy of the system (chemical, electrical, mechanical). It changes when heat passes into or out of the system, work is done on or by the system, or matter enters or leaves. U is a state function: ΔU depends only on initial and final states.' },
    { id: 'ie2', topic: '(a) Work — adiabatic', t: 'An adiabatic process is one with no transfer of heat between system and surroundings (adiabatic wall). Joule\'s experiments: doing a fixed amount of work (mechanical by paddle wheel or electrical by immersion rod) on water in a thermos flask produced the same temperature change irrespective of the way — so adiabatic work w_ad = ΔU = U₂ − U₁. Work done on the system is positive (increases U); by the system, negative.' },
    { id: 'ie3', topic: '(b) Heat', t: 'Internal energy can also change by transfer of heat q through thermally conducting walls (e.g. copper) without work, due to temperature difference. Heat absorbed by the system is positive (q > 0); heat released, negative. At constant volume with no work: ΔU = q.' },
    { id: 'ie4', topic: '(c) First law of thermodynamics', t: 'General case: ΔU = q + w (5.1). For a given change, q and w individually depend on path but q + w = ΔU is path independent. If no heat or work is transferred (isolated system), ΔU = 0. The first law: the energy of an isolated system is constant — energy can neither be created nor destroyed (law of conservation of energy).' },
    { id: 'p51', topic: 'Problems 5.1–5.2', t: 'Problem 5.1: change in internal energy when (i) no heat is absorbed but work w is done on the system — ΔU = w_ad (adiabatic); (ii) no work but q heat taken out — ΔU = −q (thermally conducting walls); (iii) w work done by the system and q heat supplied — ΔU = q − w (closed system).' },
    { id: 'pv1', topic: '5.2.1 Work — pressure-volume (Fig 5.3, 5.4)', t: 'For a gas in a cylinder with a frictionless piston, external pressure p_ex compressing the volume from Vᵢ to V_f: w = −p_ex(V_f − Vᵢ) = −p_exΔV (5.2). In compression ΔV is negative so w is positive (work on the system). For variable pressure w = −∫p_ex dV (5.3), the area under the p–V curve.' },
    { id: 'pv2', topic: 'Reversible process', t: 'A reversible process proceeds infinitely slowly through a series of equilibrium states, so the system and surroundings are always in near equilibrium; it can be reversed by an infinitesimal change. Other processes are irreversible. For reversible isothermal expansion/compression of an ideal gas (p_ex ≈ p_in = nRT/V): w_rev = −2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ) (5.6).' },
    { id: 'pv3', topic: 'Free expansion; isothermal ideal gas', t: 'Expansion of a gas into vacuum (p_ex = 0) is free expansion: no work is done, irreversibly or reversibly. For isothermal (T constant) expansion of an ideal gas into vacuum, w = 0 and q = 0, so ΔU = 0 — the internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature. For isothermal irreversible change q = −w = p_ex(V_f − Vᵢ); for isothermal reversible change q = −w = 2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ); for adiabatic change q = 0, ΔU = w_ad.' },
    { id: 'p53', topic: 'Problems 5.2–5.4', t: 'Problem 5.2: two litres of an ideal gas at 10 atm expands isothermally at 25 °C into vacuum till its volume is 10 L: free expansion, so w = 0 and q = 0 (ΔU = 0). Problem 5.3: the same expansion against a constant external pressure of 1 atm: q = −w = p_ex(8 L) = 8 L atm. Problem 5.4: the expansion carried out reversibly (isothermal): q = −w = 2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ) — more work is obtained in the reversible path.' },
    { id: 'en1', topic: '5.2.2 Enthalpy H', t: 'At constant pressure ΔU = q_p − pΔV, so q_p = (U₂ + pV₂) − (U₁ + pV₁). Enthalpy H = U + pV (5.8); q_p = ΔH (5.9). Though q is path dependent, at constant pressure it equals ΔH, a state function. ΔH = ΔU + pΔV. ΔH is negative for exothermic and positive for endothermic reactions. At constant volume q_V = ΔU.' },
    { id: 'en2', topic: 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT', t: 'For reactions involving gases: pΔV = Δn_gRT, so ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT (5.10), Δn_g = moles of gaseous products − moles of gaseous reactants. For solids and liquids the difference between ΔH and ΔU is negligible. Example: H₂O(l) → H₂O(g) at 373 K, 1 bar, ΔH = 41 kJ mol⁻¹: Δn_g = 1, ΔU = 41.00 − 3.1 = 37.9 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'ex1', topic: 'Extensive and intensive properties', t: 'An extensive property depends on the quantity or size of matter: mass, volume, internal energy, enthalpy, heat capacity. An intensive property is independent of size: temperature, density, pressure, molar heat capacity, molar volume (V/n). The ratio of two extensive properties is intensive.' },
    { id: 'hc1', topic: 'Heat capacity', t: 'Heat absorbed q = CΔT (5.12); C is the heat capacity (extensive). Molar heat capacity C_m = C/n (heat to raise 1 mol by 1 °C). Specific heat capacity c (specific heat) — heat to raise 1 g (or 1 kg) by 1 °C: q = c × m × ΔT (5.13).' },
    { id: 'hc2', topic: 'Cp − Cv = R', t: 'At constant volume q_V = C_VΔT = ΔU; at constant pressure q_p = C_pΔT = ΔH. For one mole of an ideal gas ΔH = ΔU + Δ(pV) = ΔU + RΔT, so C_pΔT = C_VΔT + RΔT, giving C_p − C_V = R (5.14).' },
    { id: 'cm1', topic: '5.3.1 Bomb calorimeter — ΔU (Fig 5.5)', t: 'Energy changes are measured by calorimetry, in a vessel (calorimeter) immersed in a known volume of liquid, using heat capacity of the liquid and calorimeter. ΔU is measured at constant volume in a bomb calorimeter: a steel vessel (bomb) immersed in a water bath; the combustible substance burns in pure oxygen; no work is done (ΔV = 0); heat released raises the temperature of water: q_V = ΔU.' },
    { id: 'cm2', topic: '5.3.2 Constant pressure calorimeter — ΔH (Fig 5.6)', t: 'ΔH (= q_p, heat absorbed or evolved at constant pressure) is measured in a calorimeter open to the atmosphere. For exothermic reactions q_p is negative (heat lost) and ΔH negative; for endothermic, positive.' },
    { id: 'p56', topic: 'Problem 5.6', t: 'Problem 5.6: 1 g graphite burnt in a bomb calorimeter in excess O₂ at 298 K, 1 atm (C(graphite) + O₂ → CO₂); temperature rises from 298 K to 299 K; heat capacity of calorimeter 20.7 kJ K⁻¹. q = −20.7 kJ per g; ΔU = −2.48 × 10² kJ mol⁻¹ (−20.7 × 12).' },
    { id: 'rh1', topic: '5.4 Reaction enthalpy ΔrH', t: 'ΔrH = Σ aᵢH(products) − Σ bᵢH(reactants) (5.15), with stoichiometric coefficients. Enthalpy of reaction depends on the conditions, so standard conditions are specified.' },
    { id: 'rh2', topic: '(a) Standard enthalpy of reaction', t: 'The standard state of a substance at a specified temperature is its pure form at 1 bar (liquid ethanol at 298 K is pure liquid at 1 bar; solid iron at 500 K is pure iron at 1 bar). Usually data are at 298 K. The standard enthalpy of reaction ΔrH° is the enthalpy change when all participants are in their standard states.' },
    { id: 'rh3', topic: '(b) Phase transformations (Table 5.1)', t: 'Standard enthalpy of fusion Δ_fusH°: enthalpy change when 1 mol of solid melts at standard state; H₂O(s) → H₂O(l): +6.00 kJ mol⁻¹. Standard enthalpy of vaporisation Δ_vapH°: 1 mol liquid vaporises at constant temperature and 1 bar; H₂O(l) → H₂O(g): +40.79 kJ mol⁻¹. Standard enthalpy of sublimation Δ_subH°: solid → vapour (CO₂ dry ice +25.2 kJ mol⁻¹ at 195 K; naphthalene +73 kJ mol⁻¹). Strong hydrogen bonding makes Δ_vapH of water high.' },
    { id: 'rh4', topic: 'Problems 5.7–5.8', t: 'Problem 5.7: a swimmer coming out of a pool has a film of water of about 18 g (1 mol); heat needed to evaporate it at 298 K (Δ_vapH° = 44.01 kJ mol⁻¹): 44.01 kJ. Problem 5.8: ΔU of vaporisation of 1 mol water at 1 bar and 100 °C (Δ_vapH = 41 kJ mol⁻¹): ΔU = ΔH − Δn_gRT = 41.00 − 3.1 = 37.9 kJ mol⁻¹ (treating vapour as ideal).' },
    { id: 'rh5', topic: '(c) Standard enthalpy of formation (Table 5.2)', t: 'The standard enthalpy change for formation of one mole of a compound from its elements in their most stable states of aggregation (reference states) is the standard molar enthalpy of formation Δ_fH°. Δ_fH° of an element in its reference state is zero (e.g. O₂(g), C(graphite), H₂(g)). Examples: H₂O(l) −285.8, CH₄(g) −74.81, C₂H₅OH(l) −277.7 kJ mol⁻¹. CaO(s) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s) is not a formation reaction (not from elements).' },
    { id: 'rh6', topic: 'ΔrH from formation enthalpies', t: 'ΔrH° = Σ aᵢΔ_fH°(products) − Σ bᵢΔ_fH°(reactants) (5.15). Example: CaCO₃(s) → CaO(s) + CO₂(g): ΔrH° = [(−635.1) + (−393.5)] − (−1206.9) = +178.3 kJ mol⁻¹ — endothermic, so decomposition requires heat.' },
    { id: 'rh7', topic: '(d) Thermochemical equations', t: 'A balanced equation with the value of ΔrH is a thermochemical equation; physical states (s, l, g, aq) are specified. Conventions: coefficients refer to moles; ΔrH° corresponds to the equation as written, in kJ mol⁻¹. If the equation is multiplied by a factor, ΔrH is multiplied likewise; reversing the equation changes the sign of ΔrH: N₂(g) + 3H₂(g) → 2NH₃(g), ΔrH° = −91.8 kJ mol⁻¹; 2NH₃(g) → N₂(g) + 3H₂(g), +91.8 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'hl1', topic: '(e) Hess\'s law (Fig 5.7)', t: 'Hess\'s law of constant heat summation: if a reaction takes place in several steps, its standard reaction enthalpy is the sum of the standard enthalpies of the intermediate reactions at the same temperature: ΔrH = ΔrH₁ + ΔrH₂ + ΔrH₃ + … (5.16). Example: C(graphite) + ½O₂ → CO(g) cannot be measured directly (CO₂ also forms); from C + O₂ → CO₂ (−393.5) and CO + ½O₂ → CO₂ (−283.0): ΔrH = −110.5 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'ce1', topic: '5.5(a) Standard enthalpy of combustion', t: 'Δ_cH° is the enthalpy change per mole of a substance when it undergoes combustion and all reactants and products are in their standard states. Combustion reactions are exothermic: butane in cooking gas C₄H₁₀(g) + 13/2 O₂ → 4CO₂ + 5H₂O(l), Δ_cH° = −2658.0 kJ mol⁻¹; glucose in the body C₆H₁₂O₆(g) + 6O₂ → 6CO₂ + 6H₂O(l), Δ_cH° = −2802.0 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'ce2', topic: 'Problem 5.9 — benzene', t: 'Problem 5.9: combustion of 1 mol benzene at 298 K and 1 atm gives CO₂(g) and H₂O(l) with 3267.0 kJ released. Using Δ_fH°(CO₂) = −393.5 and Δ_fH°(H₂O, l) = −285.83 kJ mol⁻¹: Δ_fH°(C₆H₆, l) = 6(−393.5) + 3(−285.83) + 3267.0 = 48.51 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'ae1', topic: '(b) Enthalpy of atomisation', t: 'Δ_aH° is the enthalpy change on breaking one mole of bonds completely to obtain atoms in the gas phase: H₂(g) → 2H(g), Δ_aH° = 435.0 kJ mol⁻¹; CH₄(g) → C(g) + 4H(g), 1665 kJ mol⁻¹. For diatomic molecules it equals the bond dissociation enthalpy. For metals it equals the enthalpy of sublimation: Na(s) → Na(g), 108.4 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'be1', topic: '(c) Bond enthalpy — diatomic', t: 'Bond dissociation enthalpy Δ_bondH° is the enthalpy change when one mole of covalent bonds of a gaseous covalent compound is broken to form gaseous products: H₂ 435.0, Cl₂ 242, O₂ 428 kJ mol⁻¹. Bond breaking is endothermic; bond formation exothermic. For diatomic molecules bond dissociation enthalpy = enthalpy of atomisation.' },
    { id: 'be2', topic: 'Mean bond enthalpy (Table 5.3)', t: 'In polyatomic molecules the energies of successive bond dissociations differ: in CH₄ the four C–H bonds break with different energies, so the mean bond enthalpy is used: C–H = 1665/4 = 416 kJ mol⁻¹. Mean bond enthalpies (Table 5.3): C–H 414, C–C 347, C=C 611, C≡C 837, O–H 464, N≡N 946, O=O 498 kJ mol⁻¹ (approx.). For gas-phase reactions: ΔrH° = Σ bond enthalpies of reactants − Σ bond enthalpies of products (5.17).' },
    { id: 'le1', topic: '(d) Lattice enthalpy (Fig 5.9)', t: 'The lattice enthalpy of an ionic compound is the enthalpy change when one mole of the solid separates into gaseous ions: NaCl(s) → Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g), Δ_latticeH° = +788 kJ mol⁻¹. It cannot be measured directly; it is obtained from a Born–Haber cycle using sublimation (108.4), ionisation (496), half dissociation of Cl₂ (121), electron gain (−348.6) and formation enthalpy (−411.2 kJ mol⁻¹) — Hess\'s law.' },
    { id: 'le2', topic: 'Enthalpy of solution', t: 'Enthalpy of solution Δ_solH° is the enthalpy change when one mole dissolves in a specified amount of solvent; at infinite dilution there is no further interaction between ions. Δ_solH° = Δ_latticeH° + Δ_hydH° (5.18). For most ionic compounds Δ_solH° is positive and solubility increases with temperature; if lattice enthalpy is very high (e.g. compounds of small, highly charged ions), dissolution may not occur. NaCl: Δ_solH° = 788 − 784 = 4 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'sp1', topic: '5.6 Spontaneity', t: 'A spontaneous process is one that has the potential to proceed without any outside agency (it may be very slow) — e.g. gas expanding into vacuum, heat flowing from hot to cold. Its reverse is non-spontaneous. Spontaneity does not imply instantaneous (e.g. H₂ + O₂ at room temperature is spontaneous but slow).' },
    { id: 'sp2', topic: 'Is decrease in enthalpy the criterion?', t: 'Many spontaneous reactions are exothermic (ΔH < 0): ½N₂ + 3/2 H₂ → NH₃, ΔrH° = −46.1 kJ mol⁻¹; H₂ + Cl₂ → 2HCl. But some endothermic processes are spontaneous: ½N₂ + O₂ → NO₂, ΔrH° = +33.2 kJ mol⁻¹; C(graphite) + 2S(l) → CS₂(l), +128.5 kJ mol⁻¹; dissolution of NH₄Cl. So decrease in enthalpy is a contributory factor but not the sole criterion.' },
    { id: 'en_s1', topic: 'Entropy and spontaneity (Fig 5.10)', t: 'Two gases in a closed insulated container separated by a partition mix spontaneously when the partition is removed, with no change in energy — the system becomes more disordered (more random). Entropy S is a measure of the degree of randomness or disorder of a system; isolated systems tend towards maximum entropy. For a spontaneous process in an isolated system ΔS > 0.' },
    { id: 'en_s2', topic: 'Entropy — values and ΔS = q_rev/T', t: 'Entropy is a state function. Crystalline solid has the lowest entropy, gas the highest: S(solid) < S(liquid) < S(gas). Heat added at low temperature causes more randomness than at higher temperature: ΔS = q_rev/T (5.19). Total entropy change: ΔS_total = ΔS_system + ΔS_surr > 0 for a spontaneous process (5.20); at equilibrium ΔS_total = 0.' },
    { id: 'p510', topic: 'Problems 5.10–5.11', t: 'Problem 5.10: predict entropy changes — (i) liquid crystallising into solid: decreases; (ii) temperature of crystalline solid raised from 0 K to 115 K: increases; (iii) 2NaHCO₃(s) → Na₂CO₃(s) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(g): increases; (iv) H₂(g) → 2H(g): increases. Problem 5.11: 4Fe(s) + 3O₂(g) → 2Fe₂O₃(s), ΔS = −549.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹; ΔH = −1648 × 10³ J mol⁻¹, so ΔS_surr = +5530 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ and ΔS_total = 4980.6 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ > 0 — spontaneous.' },
    { id: 'gi1', topic: 'Gibbs energy', t: 'Gibbs energy G = H − TS (5.21), an extensive state function. ΔG = ΔH − TΔS (5.22) at constant temperature (Gibbs equation). ΔH is the enthalpy (energy) factor; TΔS is the entropy (disorder) factor. ΔG gives the net energy available to do useful work — the free energy. For a spontaneous process ΔG < 0; non-spontaneous ΔG > 0; at equilibrium ΔG = 0 — because −ΔG/T = ΔS_total.' },
    { id: 'gi2', topic: 'Effect of temperature (Table 5.4)', t: 'Table 5.4: ΔH −, ΔS +: ΔG always −, spontaneous at all T. ΔH −, ΔS −: spontaneous at low T, non-spontaneous at high T. ΔH +, ΔS +: non-spontaneous at low T, spontaneous at high T. ΔH +, ΔS −: ΔG always +, non-spontaneous at all T. A reaction with ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0 becomes spontaneous above T = ΔH/ΔS.' },
    { id: 'gq1', topic: '5.7 Gibbs energy and equilibrium', t: 'For a reversible reaction at equilibrium ΔrG = 0. The standard Gibbs energy change ΔrG° = −RT ln K = −2.303 RT log K (5.23–5.25). Large negative ΔrG° gives K ≫ 1 (reaction goes almost to completion); large positive ΔrG° gives K ≪ 1. ΔrG° = ΔrH° − TΔrS° (5.26).' },
    { id: 'gq2', topic: 'Problems 5.12–5.13', t: 'Problem 5.12: ΔrG° for 3/2 O₂(g) → O₃(g) at 298 K with K_p = 2.47 × 10⁻²⁹: ΔrG° = −2.303 RT log K_p = 163 kJ mol⁻¹. Problem 5.13: equilibrium constant of 2NO₂ ⇌ N₂O₄ type reactions from ΔrG°: K = antilog(−ΔrG°/2.303RT).' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary', t: 'System, surroundings; open, closed, isolated; state functions. First law ΔU = q + w; w = −p_exΔV; reversible isothermal w = −2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ). H = U + pV; ΔH = q_p; ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT; C_p − C_V = R. Calorimetry: bomb (ΔU), constant pressure (ΔH). Standard enthalpies of formation, combustion, atomisation, bond, lattice, solution, phase changes; Hess\'s law. Spontaneity: neither ΔH nor ΔS alone; ΔS_total > 0; G = H − TS, ΔG < 0 for spontaneous change; ΔG° = −RT ln K.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 5.1–5.11', t: 'Exercises: state function — value independent of path; adiabatic: q = 0; enthalpies of elements in standard states — zero; for combustion of methane ΔH < ΔU (Δn_g = −2); Δ_fH(CH₄) from combustion data = −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹; A + B → C + D + q with ΔS > 0 is spontaneous at any temperature; ΔU = 701 − 394 = 307 J; cyanamide ΔU = −742.7 → ΔH = −741.5 kJ mol⁻¹; heat to warm 60.0 g Al by 20 °C (≈ 1.07 kJ); freezing water at 10 °C to ice at −10 °C (−7.151 kJ mol⁻¹); heat from forming 35.2 g CO₂ (−314.8 kJ).' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 5.12–5.22', t: 'Exercises: ΔrH for N₂O₄ + 3CO → N₂O + 3CO₂ (−777.7 kJ mol⁻¹); Δ_fH°(NH₃) = −46.2 kJ mol⁻¹; Δ_fH°(CH₃OH, l) = −239 kJ mol⁻¹; C–Cl bond enthalpy in CCl₄ (326 kJ mol⁻¹); isolated system ΔS > 0; reaction with ΔH = 400 kJ, ΔS = 0.2 kJ K⁻¹ spontaneous above 2000 K; 2Cl → Cl₂ has ΔH and ΔS both negative; ΔG for 2A + B → 2D (+0.164 kJ, non-spontaneous); ΔG° for K = 10 at 300 K (−5.74 kJ mol⁻¹); NO is unstable, NO₂ more stable; ΔS_surr for forming 1 mol H₂O(l) (+959.7 J K⁻¹).' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Thermodynamics deals with:', o: ['Rates of reactions', 'Energy changes in macroscopic systems', 'Mechanism of reactions', 'Atomic structure'], a: 1, e: 'Introduction.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Laws of thermodynamics apply to systems in equilibrium.', 'Thermodynamics tells how fast a reaction proceeds.'], a: 2, e: 'Not rates.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['sy1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The universe in thermodynamics is:', o: ['Only the system', 'Only the surroundings', 'System + surroundings', 'The boundary'], a: 2, e: '5.1.1.' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['sy2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 110" width="320" height="110" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="15" y="20" width="80" height="55" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="55" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">open</text><text x="55" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">matter + energy</text><rect x="120" y="20" width="80" height="55" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="160" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">closed</text><text x="160" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">energy only</text><rect x="225" y="20" width="80" height="55" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="3"/><text x="265" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">isolated</text><text x="265" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">neither</text></svg>', q: 'Reactants in a thermos flask form:', o: ['An open system', 'A closed system', 'No system', 'An isolated system'], a: 3, e: '5.1.2.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['sy2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the system with its example.', match: { h: ['System', 'Example'], l: ['Open', 'Closed', 'Isolated', 'Universe'], r: ['Open beaker with reactants', 'Closed conducting vessel', 'Thermos flask', 'System + surroundings'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 1, e: '5.1.2.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['sy3', 'xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A thermodynamic state function is a quantity:', o: ['Whose value is independent of path', 'Used to determine heat changes', 'Used to determine p-V work', 'Depending on temperature only'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.1.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['sy3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Work and heat are state functions.', 'Internal energy and enthalpy are state functions.'], a: 3, e: 'Path functions.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['ie1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'ΔU depends only on initial and final states.', R: 'Internal energy is a state function.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['ie2', 'xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For a process under adiabatic conditions:', o: ['ΔT = 0', 'q = 0', 'Δp = 0', 'w = 0'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.2.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['ie2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Work done on the system is taken as positive.', 'In Joule\'s experiments, different ways of doing the same adiabatic work gave different temperature changes.'], a: 2, e: 'Same change.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['ie3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Heat absorbed by the system is positive.', 'At constant volume with no work, ΔU = q.'], a: 0, e: '5.1.4(b).' },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['ie4', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: '701 J of heat is absorbed by a system and 394 J of work is done by it. ΔU is:', o: ['1095 J', '307 J', '−307 J', '394 J'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.7.', sol: ['ΔU = q + w', 'q = +701 J, w = −394 J', 'ΔU = 701 − 394', 'Answer: 307 J'] },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['ie4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'For an isolated system ΔU = 0.', R: 'No heat or work is exchanged with the surroundings.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['p51'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For a closed system where w work is done by the system and q heat supplied, ΔU is:', o: ['q + w', '−q − w', 'q − w', 'w − q'], a: 2, e: 'Problem 5.1.' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['pv1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 260 140" width="260" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="40" y="30" width="140" height="90" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="150" y="30" width="10" height="90" fill="#D9A520" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" fill-opacity="0.6"/><line x1="230" y1="75" x2="165" y2="75" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="165,75 170.4,72.4 170.4,77.6" fill="currentColor"/><text x="235" y="70" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">p_ex</text><text x="95" y="80" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">gas</text><line x1="40" y1="132" x2="150" y2="132" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,132 146.4,133.7 146.4,130.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="95" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">V</text></svg>', q: 'A gas expands against constant external pressure 2 atm from 5 L to 10 L. Work done is:', o: ['+10 L atm', '−20 L atm', '−10 L atm', '−5 L atm'], a: 2, e: 'w = −p_exΔV.', sol: ['w = −p_exΔV', '= −2 × (10 − 5)', '= −10 L atm', 'Answer: −10 L atm (≈ −1013 J)'] },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['pv1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 180" width="280" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="154" x2="276" y2="154" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="276,154 269.7,157 269.7,151" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="156" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="274" y="148" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">V</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">p</text><line x1="85.2" y1="151" x2="85.2" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="85.2" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Vᵢ</text><line x1="220.8" y1="151" x2="220.8" y2="157" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="220.8" y="168" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">V_f</text><text x="34" y="167" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><polyline points="78.4,22.2 80.6,29.4 82.8,35.8 85,41.6 87.2,46.8 89.4,51.6 91.6,56 93.8,60 96,63.7 98.3,67.1 100.5,70.3 102.7,73.2 104.9,76 107.1,78.5 109.3,80.9 111.5,83.2 113.7,85.3 115.9,87.3 118.1,89.2 120.3,90.9 122.5,92.6 124.7,94.2 126.9,95.7 129.1,97.2 131.3,98.6 133.5,99.9 135.7,101.1 137.9,102.3 140.1,103.4 142.3,104.5 144.5,105.6 146.7,106.6 148.9,107.5 151.1,108.4 153.3,109.3 155.5,110.2 157.7,111 159.9,111.8 162.2,112.6 164.4,113.3 166.6,114 168.8,114.7 171,115.3 173.2,116 175.4,116.6 177.6,117.2 179.8,117.8 182,118.3 184.2,118.9 186.4,119.4 188.6,119.9 190.8,120.4 193,120.9 195.2,121.4 197.4,121.8 199.6,122.3 201.8,122.7 204,123.1 206.2,123.5 208.4,123.9 210.6,124.3 212.8,124.7 215,125.1 217.2,125.4 219.4,125.8 221.6,126.1 223.9,126.5 226.1,126.8 228.3,127.1 230.5,127.4 232.7,127.7 234.9,128 237.1,128.3 239.3,128.6 241.5,128.9 243.7,129.1 245.9,129.4 248.1,129.7 250.3,129.9 252.5,130.2 254.7,130.4" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><line x1="85.2" y1="154" x2="85.2" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><line x1="220.8" y1="154" x2="220.8" y2="126" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="153" y="84" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">w = −∫p dV</text></svg>', st: ['In compression ΔV is negative and w is positive.', 'Work done is the area under the p–V curve.'], a: 0, e: '5.2.1.' },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['pv2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For reversible isothermal expansion of an ideal gas, work done is:', o: ['−p_ex(V_f − Vᵢ)', 'Zero', 'nC_VΔT', '−2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ)'], a: 3, e: 'Eq. 5.6.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['pv2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Maximum work is obtained in a reversible expansion.', R: 'In a reversible process the external pressure is always only infinitesimally less than the internal pressure.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['pv3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'An ideal gas expands into vacuum isothermally. The work done is:', o: ['nRT', '−p_exΔV with p_ex = 1 atm', 'Infinite', 'Zero'], a: 3, e: 'Free expansion.', sol: ['p_ex = 0', 'w = −0 × ΔV', '= 0', 'Answer: zero (ΔU = 0, q = 0)'] },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['pv3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'ΔU = 0 for isothermal expansion of an ideal gas.', R: 'Internal energy of an ideal gas depends only on temperature.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['p53'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: '2 L of ideal gas at 10 atm expands isothermally against constant 1 atm to 10 L (Problem 5.3). Work done is:', o: ['−8 L atm', '−16.1 L atm', '0', '−10 L atm'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 5.3.', sol: ['w = −p_ex(V_f − Vᵢ)', '= −1 × (10 − 2)', '= −8 L atm', 'Answer: −8 L atm (−810.4 J)'] },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['p53'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: '2 L of ideal gas at 10 atm (pV = 20 L atm) expands reversibly and isothermally to 10 L. Work done is about:', o: ['−8 L atm', '−16.1 L atm', '−20 L atm', '−32.2 L atm'], a: 1, e: 'w = −2.303 nRT log(V_f/Vᵢ).', sol: ['nRT = pV = 10 × 2 = 20 L atm', 'w = −2.303 × 20 × log 5', '= −46.06 × 0.699', 'Answer: ≈ −16.1 L atm'] },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['en1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'At constant pressure, heat absorbed equals:', o: ['ΔH', 'ΔU', 'w', 'ΔG'], a: 0, e: 'q_p = ΔH.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['en1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For an exothermic reaction ΔH is positive.', 'Enthalpy H = U + pV.'], a: 3, e: 'Negative.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['en2', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For NH₂CN(s) + 3/2 O₂(g) → N₂(g) + CO₂(g) + H₂O(l), ΔU = −742.7 kJ mol⁻¹ at 298 K. ΔH is:', o: ['−743.9 kJ mol⁻¹', '−742.7 kJ mol⁻¹', '−741.5 kJ mol⁻¹', '−745.2 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.8.', sol: ['Δn_g = 2 − 1.5 = +0.5', 'Δn_gRT = 0.5 × 8.314 × 10⁻³ × 298 = 1.24 kJ', 'ΔH = −742.7 + 1.24', 'Answer: −741.5 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['en2', 'xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'ΔU of combustion of methane is −X kJ mol⁻¹. ΔH is:', o: ['= ΔU', '< ΔU', '> ΔU', '= 0'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.4: Δn_g = −2.' },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['en2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For H₂O(l) → H₂O(g) at 373 K, ΔH = 41 kJ mol⁻¹. ΔU is about:', o: ['41 kJ mol⁻¹', '44.1 kJ mol⁻¹', '3.1 kJ mol⁻¹', '37.9 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Problem 5.8.', sol: ['Δn_g = 1', 'RT = 8.314 × 373 = 3.1 kJ', 'ΔU = 41 − 3.1', 'Answer: 37.9 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['ex1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which are intensive properties?', s: ['Temperature', 'Density', 'Molar heat capacity', 'Enthalpy'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'Enthalpy is extensive.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['ex1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Molar volume is an intensive property.', R: 'The ratio of two extensive properties is intensive.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['hc1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Heat needed to raise 60.0 g Al from 35 °C to 55 °C (C_m = 24 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹) is about:', o: ['1.07 kJ', '2.4 kJ', '0.48 kJ', '28.8 kJ'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.9.', sol: ['n = 60/27 = 2.22 mol', 'q = nC_mΔT = 2.22 × 24 × 20', '= 1066 J', 'Answer: ≈ 1.07 kJ'] },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['hc1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Heat capacity is an extensive property.', 'Specific heat refers to 1 mol of a substance.'], a: 2, e: 'To unit mass.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['hc2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For an ideal gas, C_p − C_V equals:', o: ['0', 'R/2', 'R', '2R'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 5.14.' },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['hc2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'C_p of an ideal gas is greater than C_V.', R: 'At constant pressure extra heat is needed for expansion work.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['cm1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 220 160" width="220" height="160" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><rect x="20" y="20" width="180" height="120" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><rect x="70" y="50" width="80" height="70" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" rx="10" fill-opacity="0.25"/><text x="110" y="90" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">bomb (O₂)</text><text x="40" y="40" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">water</text><line x1="170" y1="5" x2="170" y2="100" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2"/><text x="176" y="12" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">thermometer</text><text x="110" y="155" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔV = 0 → q_V = ΔU</text></svg>', q: 'A bomb calorimeter measures:', o: ['ΔH', 'ΔG', 'ΔS', 'ΔU'], a: 3, e: '5.3.1.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['cm1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Heat measured in a bomb calorimeter equals ΔU.', R: 'The bomb is sealed; volume is constant and no work is done.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['cm2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For endothermic reactions measured at constant pressure, q_p is negative.', 'ΔH is measured in a calorimeter at constant pressure.'], a: 3, e: 'Positive.' },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['p56'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: '1 g graphite burnt in a bomb calorimeter raises temperature by 1 K; heat capacity of the calorimeter is 20.7 kJ K⁻¹. ΔU per mole of graphite is about:', o: ['−20.7 kJ mol⁻¹', '−248 kJ mol⁻¹', '−393.5 kJ mol⁻¹', '+248 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 5.6.', sol: ['q = −C ΔT = −20.7 kJ per g', 'Per mol = −20.7 × 12', '= −248.4 kJ', 'Answer: ≈ −2.48 × 10² kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['rh1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'ΔrH equals:', o: ['Σ H(products) − Σ H(reactants)', 'Σ H(reactants) − Σ H(products)', 'Σ H(products) + Σ H(reactants)', 'ΔU − pΔV'], a: 0, e: 'Eq. 5.15.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['rh2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The standard state of a substance is its pure form at:', o: ['1 atm and 0 °C', '1 bar', '0 K', '2 bar'], a: 1, e: '5.4(a).' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['rh3', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Enthalpy change on freezing 1 mol water at 10 °C to ice at −10 °C (C_p liquid 75.3, solid 36.8 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹; Δ_fusH 6.03 kJ mol⁻¹):', o: ['−6.03 kJ mol⁻¹', '+7.151 kJ mol⁻¹', '−7.151 kJ mol⁻¹', '−5.65 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.10.', sol: ['Cool water 10 → 0 °C: −0.753 kJ', 'Freeze: −6.03 kJ', 'Cool ice 0 → −10 °C: −0.368 kJ', 'Answer: −7.151 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['rh3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Δ_vapH of water is high.', R: 'Strong hydrogen bonds hold water molecules in the liquid.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['rh4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Heat needed to evaporate 18 g of water at 298 K (Δ_vapH° = 44.01 kJ mol⁻¹):', o: ['22.0 kJ', '792 kJ', '2.44 kJ', '44.01 kJ'], a: 3, e: 'Problem 5.7.', sol: ['18 g = 1 mol', 'q = n Δ_vapH°', '= 1 × 44.01', 'Answer: 44.01 kJ'] },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['rh5', 'xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The standard enthalpies of formation of all elements in their reference states are:', o: ['Unity', 'Negative', 'Zero', 'Different for each element'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.3.' },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['rh5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which is a formation reaction?', o: ['CaO(s) + CO₂(g) → CaCO₃(s)', 'CO(g) + ½O₂ → CO₂(g)', '2H₂O(l) → 2H₂ + O₂', 'C(graphite) + 2H₂(g) → CH₄(g)'], a: 3, e: '5.4(c).' },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['rh5', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For N₂ + 3H₂ → 2NH₃, ΔrH = −92.4 kJ mol⁻¹. Δ_fH° of NH₃ is:', o: ['−46.2 kJ mol⁻¹', '−92.4 kJ mol⁻¹', '+46.2 kJ mol⁻¹', '−184.8 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.13.', sol: ['Δ_fH° refers to 1 mol NH₃', '= −92.4/2', '= −46.2', 'Answer: −46.2 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['rh6'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For CaCO₃ → CaO + CO₂ with Δ_fH°: CaCO₃ −1206.9, CaO −635.1, CO₂ −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹, ΔrH° is:', o: ['−178.3 kJ mol⁻¹', '+178.3 kJ mol⁻¹', '+2235.5 kJ mol⁻¹', '−1028.6 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 1, e: '5.4(c).', sol: ['ΔrH° = Σ products − Σ reactants', '= (−635.1 − 393.5) − (−1206.9)', '= +178.3', 'Answer: +178.3 kJ mol⁻¹ (endothermic)'] },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['rh6', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'ΔrH for N₂O₄ + 3CO → N₂O + 3CO₂ (Δ_fH: CO −110, CO₂ −393, N₂O 81, N₂O₄ 9.7 kJ mol⁻¹):', o: ['+777.7 kJ mol⁻¹', '−403.7 kJ mol⁻¹', '−777.7 kJ mol⁻¹', '−1189 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.12.', sol: ['Products: 81 + 3(−393) = −1098', 'Reactants: 9.7 + 3(−110) = −320.3', 'ΔrH = −1098 + 320.3', 'Answer: −777.7 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['rh7'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Reversing a thermochemical equation reverses the sign of ΔrH.', R: 'Enthalpy is a state function.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['rh7'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['If an equation is multiplied by 2, ΔrH is doubled.', 'Physical states need not be specified in thermochemical equations.'], a: 2, e: 'They must be.' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['hl1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 140" width="300" height="140" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><text x="40" y="30" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">C + O₂</text><text x="260" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">CO₂</text><text x="150" y="120" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">CO + ½O₂</text><line x1="60" y1="40" x2="245" y2="110" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="245,110 239,110.5 240.9,105.6" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="170" y="60" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔH = −393.5</text><line x1="45" y1="40" x2="130" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="130,110 124.2,108.6 127.5,104.6" fill="currentColor"/><text x="55" y="90" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔH₁ = −110.5</text><line x1="185" y1="120" x2="240" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="240,120 234.6,122.6 234.6,117.4" fill="currentColor"/><text x="212" y="136" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">−283.0</text></svg>', q: 'C + O₂ → CO₂ (−393.5) and CO + ½O₂ → CO₂ (−283.0 kJ mol⁻¹). ΔrH for C + ½O₂ → CO is:', o: ['−676.5 kJ mol⁻¹', '+110.5 kJ mol⁻¹', '−283.0 kJ mol⁻¹', '−110.5 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Hess\'s law.', sol: ['ΔH = ΔH₁ − ΔH₂', '= −393.5 − (−283.0)', '= −110.5', 'Answer: −110.5 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['hl1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Combustion enthalpies of CH₄, graphite, H₂ are −890.3, −393.5, −285.8 kJ mol⁻¹. Δ_fH(CH₄) is:', o: ['−74.8 kJ mol⁻¹', '−52.27 kJ mol⁻¹', '+74.8 kJ mol⁻¹', '+52.26 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.5.', sol: ['Δ_fH = Δ_cH(C) + 2Δ_cH(H₂) − Δ_cH(CH₄)', '= −393.5 − 571.6 + 890.3', '= −74.8', 'Answer: −74.8 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['hl1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Δ_fH°(CH₃OH, l) from: CH₃OH + 3/2 O₂ → CO₂ + 2H₂O(l), −726; C + O₂ → CO₂, −393; H₂ + ½O₂ → H₂O(l), −286 kJ mol⁻¹:', o: ['+239 kJ mol⁻¹', '−239 kJ mol⁻¹', '−47 kJ mol⁻¹', '−1405 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.14.', sol: ['Δ_fH = −393 + 2(−286) − (−726)', '= −393 − 572 + 726', '= −239', 'Answer: −239 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['hl1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Hess\'s law allows calculation of enthalpies that cannot be measured directly.', R: 'Enthalpy is a state function.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['ce1', 'xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Δ_cH(C) = −393.5 kJ mol⁻¹. Heat released on forming 35.2 g CO₂ is:', o: ['393.5 kJ', '157.4 kJ', '314.8 kJ', '1386 kJ'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.11.', sol: ['n(CO₂) = 35.2/44 = 0.8 mol', 'q = 0.8 × 393.5', '= 314.8', 'Answer: 314.8 kJ released'] },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['ce1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Δ_cH° of glucose is about +2802 kJ mol⁻¹.', 'Combustion reactions are exothermic.'], a: 3, e: '−2802 kJ mol⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['ce2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Benzene burns releasing 3267.0 kJ mol⁻¹. With Δ_fH°(CO₂) = −393.5, Δ_fH°(H₂O, l) = −285.83 kJ mol⁻¹, Δ_fH°(C₆H₆, l) is:', o: ['−48.51 kJ mol⁻¹', '3267 kJ mol⁻¹', '−3218 kJ mol⁻¹', '48.51 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Problem 5.9.', sol: ['6(−393.5) = −2361', '3(−285.83) = −857.49', 'Δ_fH = −2361 − 857.49 + 3267', 'Answer: 48.51 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['ae1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For metals like sodium, the enthalpy of atomisation equals:', o: ['Enthalpy of sublimation', 'Enthalpy of fusion', 'Ionisation enthalpy', 'Lattice enthalpy'], a: 0, e: '5.5(b).' },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['ae1', 'be2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Enthalpy of atomisation of CH₄ is 1665 kJ mol⁻¹. The mean C–H bond enthalpy is:', o: ['416 kJ mol⁻¹', '1665 kJ mol⁻¹', '832 kJ mol⁻¹', '555 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 0, e: '5.5(c).', sol: ['Four C–H bonds', '1665/4', '= 416.25', 'Answer: ≈ 416 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['be1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Bond dissociation is endothermic.', R: 'Energy must be supplied to break a bond.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['be2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For a gas-phase reaction, ΔrH° in terms of bond enthalpies is:', o: ['Σ products − Σ reactants', 'Σ(bond enthalpies of reactants) − Σ(bond enthalpies of products)', 'Σ reactants + Σ products', 'Zero'], a: 1, e: 'Eq. 5.17.' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['be2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For CCl₄(g) → C(g) + 4Cl(g), ΔH = 1304 kJ mol⁻¹. The C–Cl bond enthalpy is:', o: ['1304 kJ mol⁻¹', '326 kJ mol⁻¹', '652 kJ mol⁻¹', '242 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.15.', sol: ['Four C–Cl bonds', '1304/4', '= 326', 'Answer: 326 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['le1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 320 200" width="320" height="200" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="30" y1="180" x2="170" y2="180" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="176" y="184" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na(s) + ½Cl₂(g)</text><line x1="30" y1="150" x2="170" y2="150" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="176" y="154" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na(g) + ½Cl₂(g)</text><line x1="30" y1="120" x2="170" y2="120" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="176" y="124" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na(g) + Cl(g)</text><line x1="30" y1="40" x2="170" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="176" y="44" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na⁺(g) + e⁻ + Cl(g)</text><line x1="30" y1="70" x2="170" y2="70" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1"/><text x="176" y="74" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">Na⁺(g) + Cl⁻(g)</text><line x1="40" y1="180" x2="40" y2="152" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,152 41.7,155.6 38.3,155.6" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="55" y1="150" x2="55" y2="122" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="55,122 56.7,125.6 53.3,125.6" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="70" y1="120" x2="70" y2="42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="70,42 71.7,45.6 68.3,45.6" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="90" y1="42" x2="90" y2="68" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="90,68 88.3,64.4 91.7,64.4" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="150" y1="70" x2="150" y2="178" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="150,178 147.8,173.5 152.2,173.5" fill="#D9A520"/><text x="140" y="130" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">lattice (−788)</text></svg>', q: 'The lattice enthalpy of NaCl is about:', o: ['−411 kJ mol⁻¹', '+496 kJ mol⁻¹', '+788 kJ mol⁻¹', '+108 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: '5.5(d).' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['le1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Lattice enthalpy is determined using a Born–Haber cycle.', R: 'It cannot be measured directly experimentally.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['le2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For NaCl, lattice enthalpy is +788 and hydration enthalpy −784 kJ mol⁻¹. Δ_solH° is:', o: ['−4 kJ mol⁻¹', '+1572 kJ mol⁻¹', '+4 kJ mol⁻¹', '−1572 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 2, e: 'Eq. 5.18.', sol: ['Δ_solH° = Δ_latticeH° + Δ_hydH°', '= 788 + (−784)', '= +4', 'Answer: +4 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['le2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['For most ionic compounds Δ_solH° is positive.', 'Solubility of most ionic compounds decreases with temperature.'], a: 2, e: 'Increases.' },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['sp1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'A spontaneous process is one that:', o: ['Is always instantaneous', 'Always absorbs heat', 'Never reverses', 'Can proceed without outside agency'], a: 3, e: '5.6.' },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['sp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A mixture of H₂ and O₂ at room temperature does not react noticeably.', R: 'Spontaneity does not mean the reaction is fast.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['sp2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Decrease in enthalpy is not the sole criterion for spontaneity.', R: 'Some endothermic reactions, like dissolution of NH₄Cl, are spontaneous.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['sp2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['All spontaneous reactions are exothermic.', 'Formation of NH₃ from N₂ and H₂ is exothermic.'], a: 3, e: 'Not all.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['en_s1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Two gases mix spontaneously in an insulated container.', R: 'Mixing increases the randomness (entropy) of the system.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['en_s1', 'xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For an isolated system with ΔU = 0, a spontaneous change has:', o: ['ΔS > 0', 'ΔS < 0', 'ΔS = 0', 'ΔH > 0'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.16.' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['en_s2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Δ_fH°(H₂O, l) = −286 kJ mol⁻¹ at 298 K. ΔS of surroundings when 1 mol H₂O(l) forms is:', o: ['−959.7 J K⁻¹', '+0.96 J K⁻¹', '+286 J K⁻¹', '+959.7 J K⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 5.22.', sol: ['q_surr = +286000 J', 'ΔS_surr = q/T', '= 286000/298', 'Answer: +959.7 J K⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['en_s2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Entropy of a gas is higher than that of its solid.', 'At equilibrium ΔS_total is maximum and positive.'], a: 2, e: 'ΔS_total = 0 at equilibrium.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['p510'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Entropy decreases when:', o: ['H₂(g) → 2H(g)', 'A liquid crystallises into a solid', 'Temperature of a solid is raised', 'NaHCO₃ decomposes giving gases'], a: 1, e: 'Problem 5.10.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['p510'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For 4Fe + 3O₂ → 2Fe₂O₃: ΔS = −549.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹, ΔH = −1648 kJ mol⁻¹ at 298 K. ΔS_total is about:', o: ['+4980 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '−549.4 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '+5530 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹', '−4980 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 5.11.', sol: ['ΔS_surr = 1648 × 10³/298 = 5530', 'ΔS_total = 5530 − 549.4', '= 4980.6', 'Answer: +4980 J K⁻¹ mol⁻¹ (spontaneous)'] },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['gi1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'For a spontaneous process at constant T and p:', o: ['ΔG > 0', 'ΔG = 0', 'ΔG < 0', 'ΔS < 0'], a: 2, e: '5.6.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['gi1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For 2A(g) + B(g) → 2D(g), ΔU = −10.5 kJ and ΔS = −44.1 J K⁻¹ at 298 K. ΔG is about:', o: ['−0.16 kJ', '+0.16 kJ (non-spontaneous)', '−25.6 kJ', '+13.1 kJ'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.19.', sol: ['Δn_g = −1; ΔH = −10.5 − 2.48 = −12.98 kJ', 'TΔS = 298 × (−0.0441) = −13.14 kJ', 'ΔG = −12.98 + 13.14', 'Answer: ≈ +0.16 kJ'] },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['gi1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'ΔG gives the useful work available from a process.', R: 'G = H − TS combines energy and entropy factors.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R is the definition.' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['gi2', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, tag: '📈 Graph', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 280 180" width="280" height="180" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="38" y1="84" x2="276" y2="84" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="276,84 269.7,87 269.7,81" fill="currentColor"/><line x1="40" y1="156" x2="40" y2="10" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><polygon points="40,10 43,16.3 37,16.3" fill="currentColor"/><text x="274" y="78" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">T</text><text x="46" y="18" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔG</text><text x="34" y="97" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><polyline points="40,42 254.7,135.1" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="137.2" cy="84" r="3" fill="currentColor" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="143.2" y="76" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">T = ΔH/ΔS</text><text x="62.6" y="133" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ΔH > 0, ΔS > 0</text></svg>', q: 'For 2A + B → C, ΔH = 400 kJ mol⁻¹ and ΔS = 0.2 kJ K⁻¹ mol⁻¹. It becomes spontaneous above:', o: ['200 K', '80 K', '2000 K', '400 K'], a: 2, e: 'Exercise 5.17.', sol: ['ΔG = 0 at T = ΔH/ΔS', '= 400/0.2', '= 2000 K', 'Answer: above 2000 K'] },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['gi2', 'xe1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'A + B → C + D + q has ΔS > 0. The reaction will be:', o: ['Possible only at high temperature', 'Possible only at low temperature', 'Not possible at any temperature', 'Possible at any temperature'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 5.6: ΔH < 0, ΔS > 0.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['gi2'], type: 'match', lvl: 3, q: 'Match signs of ΔH and ΔS with spontaneity (Table 5.4).', match: { h: ['ΔH, ΔS', 'Spontaneity'], l: ['−, +', '−, −', '+, +', '+, −'], r: ['All temperatures', 'Low temperature only', 'High temperature only', 'Never'] }, o: ['A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV', 'A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I'], a: 2, e: 'Table 5.4.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['gi2', 'xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'For 2Cl(g) → Cl₂(g), the signs of ΔH and ΔS are:', o: ['Both negative', 'Both positive', 'ΔH −, ΔS +', 'ΔH +, ΔS −'], a: 0, e: 'Exercise 5.18.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['gq1', 'xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The equilibrium constant of a reaction is 10 at 300 K. ΔG° is about:', o: ['+5.74 kJ mol⁻¹', '−2.49 kJ mol⁻¹', '−57.4 kJ mol⁻¹', '−5.74 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 3, e: 'Exercise 5.20.', sol: ['ΔG° = −2.303 RT log K', '= −2.303 × 8.314 × 300 × 1', '= −5744 J', 'Answer: −5.74 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['gq1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A reaction with large negative ΔrG° goes nearly to completion.', R: 'ΔrG° = −RT ln K, so K ≫ 1.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['gq2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'For 3/2 O₂ → O₃ at 298 K, K_p = 2.47 × 10⁻²⁹. ΔrG° is about:', o: ['+163 kJ mol⁻¹', '−163 kJ mol⁻¹', '+71 kJ mol⁻¹', '+16.3 kJ mol⁻¹'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 5.12.', sol: ['log K_p = −28.6', 'ΔG° = −2.303 × 8.314 × 298 × (−28.6)', '= 1.63 × 10⁵ J', 'Answer: ≈ +163 kJ mol⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['su1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['C_p − C_V = 2R for an ideal gas.', 'At equilibrium ΔG = 0.'], a: 3, e: '= R.' },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['xe2', 'rh5'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'N₂ + O₂ → NO has ΔrH = +90 kJ mol⁻¹ (per ½N₂ + ½O₂). Hence NO is:', o: ['Very stable', 'Thermodynamically unstable', 'An element', 'Formed spontaneously at all T'], a: 1, e: 'Exercise 5.21.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['sy2', 'sy3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A closed system can exchange matter with the surroundings.', R: 'A closed system exchanges only energy.' }, a: 3, e: 'A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['ie3', 'ie4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Heat is a state function.', R: 'q + w = ΔU is path independent.' }, a: 3, e: 'q is a path function; A false.' },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['pv1', 'pv3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Work is done in free expansion of a gas.', R: 'In free expansion p_ex = 0.' }, a: 3, e: 'No work; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['en1', 'cm1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'ΔH and ΔU are always equal.', R: 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT.' }, a: 3, e: 'Differ when Δn_g ≠ 0; A false.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['rh5', 'rh6'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Δ_fH° of O₃(g) is zero.', R: 'Δ_fH° of an element in its reference state is zero.' }, a: 3, e: 'O₃ is not the reference state (O₂ is); A false.' },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['gi2', 'gi1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A reaction with ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0 is spontaneous at high temperature.', R: 'ΔG = ΔH − TΔS.' }, a: 3, e: 'Never spontaneous; A false, R true.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['en_s2', 'sp1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Entropy is a state function.', R: 'Spontaneous processes in isolated systems increase entropy.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['hc2', 'ex1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Heat capacity is extensive.', R: 'C_p − C_V = R for one mole of ideal gas.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; unrelated.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['le1', 'ae1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Enthalpy of atomisation of H₂ is 435 kJ mol⁻¹.', R: 'Lattice enthalpy of NaCl is 788 kJ mol⁻¹.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; unrelated.' },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['ce1', 'cm2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Combustion enthalpies are negative.', R: 'Combustion releases heat.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['ie2', 'pv2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In an adiabatic process ΔT = 0.', 'An irreversible process passes through a series of equilibrium states.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['hl1', 'rh7'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Hess\'s law applies only to reactions occurring in one step.', 'Reversing an equation leaves ΔrH unchanged.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['gq1', 'gi1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['ΔG° = +RT ln K.', 'At equilibrium ΔG is negative.'], a: 1, e: 'Both false.' },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['sy1'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['The boundary separates the system from the surroundings.', 'Surroundings include only the portion of the universe that cannot interact with the system.'], a: 2, e: 'That can interact.' },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['ie1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Internal energy is a path function.', 'Internal energy changes when matter enters or leaves the system.'], a: 3, e: 'State function.' },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['p51'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['If no heat is absorbed but work w is done on the system, ΔU = w_ad.', 'If q heat is taken out with no work, ΔU = +q.'], a: 2, e: 'ΔU = −q.' },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['p56'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Problem 5.6 the combustion was carried out at constant volume.', 'The heat released per gram of graphite was 20.7 kJ.'], a: 0, e: 'Problem 5.6.' },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['rh1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['ΔrH is independent of physical states of reactants and products.', 'ΔrH depends on the stoichiometric coefficients.'], a: 3, e: 'Depends on states.' },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['rh2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Standard state of liquid ethanol at 298 K is pure liquid at 1 bar.', 'Standard state pressure is 1 atm in current convention.'], a: 2, e: '1 bar.' },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['rh4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In Problem 5.8, ΔU of vaporisation is greater than ΔH.', 'Evaporation of the water film cools a swimmer.'], a: 3, e: 'ΔU < ΔH (Δn_g = +1).' },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['ce2'], type: 'st', lvl: 3, st: ['In Problem 5.9, Δ_fH° of benzene is positive.', 'Benzene combustion is endothermic.'], a: 2, e: 'Exothermic (−3267 kJ).' },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['be1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Bond formation is endothermic.', 'For diatomic molecules, bond dissociation enthalpy equals atomisation enthalpy.'], a: 3, e: 'Exothermic.' },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['gq2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A large positive ΔrG° means K ≪ 1.', 'Conversion of O₂ to O₃ at 298 K is highly favourable.'], a: 2, e: 'ΔrG° = +163 kJ; unfavourable.' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['su1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which relation is correct?', o: ['ΔU = ΔH + Δn_gRT', 'ΔG = ΔH + TΔS', 'ΔH = ΔU + Δn_gRT', 'q_V = ΔH'], a: 2, e: 'Summary.' }
  ]
});
